# CODEOWNERS — minimal money-path hard gate.
#
# Only DB migrations require a code-owner review in GitHub. Migrations are
# irreversible in production, so they keep an independent second approval (from
# a collaborator other than the PR author — GitHub's self-approval rule).
#
# All OTHER money-path changes (x402 / payments / machine-payments /
# payment-coverage / allowance-module / agentAuth / release tooling) are NOT
# hard-gated here, and as of #1024 they are not gated in-session either.
#
# The pause that used to exist applied ONLY to PRs opened through ship-next; a
# hand-written money-path PR merged on green CI alone. That made the canonical
# workflow more expensive than bypassing it while protecting nothing on the
# bypass path — and the "approval" was usually the PR author's own, which is
# exactly what GitHub disallows for the migrations gate above.
#
# What protects the money path now is automatic and tool-independent:
#   1. this file, for irreversible schema changes;
#   2. the `qa-freshness` job in .github/workflows/dev-gate.yml, which refuses a
#      dev -> main promotion without a recent GREEN money-flow QA run.
#
# That second one is load-bearing. It only bites while it is listed in `main`'s
# required status checks — if it is ever removed from the ruleset, restore it
# here or widen the paths below.
#
# See docs/contributing/autonomous-pr-loop.md ("Money-path safety model").

